event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_0182aa178768d8c9006a7395ea3254819494bfee6a526cee9d","logprobs":[],"output_index":4,"sequence_number":337,"text":"The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA/JPL reported on Aug. 5, 2026, that Perseverance captured a first-of-its-kind image sequence from Mars: Earth disappearing behind the Martian moon Phobos. The Mastcam-Z camera took the images on July 2, 2026 — sol 1,907 of the mission — from the rim of Jezero Crater, showing Earth as a tiny bright dot being occulted by Phobos in the Martian evening sky【5:0†source】.\n\nOther recent Perseverance updates:\n\n- In mid-July 2026, NASA reported that Perseverance had investigated the “Broom Point member,” a 245-foot-thick stack of ancient rock on Jezero Crater’s rim. The rover found evidence that the rocks were built up by repeated asteroid impacts more than 3.9 billion years ago【5:1†source】.\n- Perseverance also recently reached a driving milestone: it completed a “marathon” on Mars, totaling 26.2 miles, or 42.195 km, on sol 1,890. It achieved that distance in about five years and four months, much faster than Opportunity reached the same milestone【5:2†source】.\n- Earlier in 2026, NASA reported that Perseverance successfully completed the first AI-planned drives on Mars, using AI-generated waypoints rather than routes planned entirely by human rover operators【5:4†source】.\n\nIn short: the newest headline is the Phobos-Earth occultation image, while Perseverance is also continuing major geological science beyond Jezero Crater and setting mobility/autonomy milestones."}

